3 #uuid "9610f5eb-4c2a-5356-ab60-5e12da9a7560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162463 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162463 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162463 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162463 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162463 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221873" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162463 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162463 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe notation $3∤8$ means {{field-1}}. It is equivalent to the statement that {{field-2}}{{field-3}} $k,$ we have {{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162463 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "116c50e1-51bc-58dd-a9dc-8ed9be12bea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162463 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∤b$ means $a$ does not divides $b.$ So, $3∤8$ means $3\\text{ doesn't divide }8.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb\\ne ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3∤8$ is equivalent to the statement that $\\text{for any}$ $\\text{integer}$ $k,$ we have $8\\ne 3k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162734 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162734 #edb/ref 1061 162161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162734 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b0fa360f-9e02-5a70-b17f-90340c91003c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162734 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162735 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162735 #edb/ref 1061 162162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162735 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3902c14b-dd71-5c37-bd20-4fb2177d36a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162735 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162736 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162736 #edb/ref 1061 162166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162736 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3ae5e0bc-75a2-5dd9-b814-d4bdb58d123f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162736 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162737 #edb/ref 1061 162169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162737 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d795623f-e963-5223-922d-0146dbda23bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162737 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162738 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162738 #edb/ref 1061 162181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162738 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7cc84b4a-0d80-572a-b21b-ca0aa54f80ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162738 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163367 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose $a$ and $b$ are integers where $a\\neq 0.$ We say that $\\boldsymbol a$ **divides** $\\boldsymbol b$ and write \n\n$$\na\\mid b\n$$\n\nif there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\nb = ka.\n$$\n\nWe can write this more formally as follows:\n\n$$\na\\mid b \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\exists k \\in \\mathbb Z, b = ka\n$$\n\nFor example, $2\\mid 8$ because there exists an integer ${\\color{blue}{k}} = {\\color{blue}{4}}$ such that\n\n$$\n8 = {\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot 2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163367 #edb/ref 1065 9351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163367 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "100e1a89-de92-5d1a-86ba-bcd72190ae6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163367 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163368 #edb/ref 1064 "We have the following definition of integer divisibility. \n\n$$\na\\mid b \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\exists k \\in \\mathbb Z, b = ka\n$$\n\nIf $a$ **does not divide** $b,$ we write $a\\not\\mid b.$ Therefore, by negating the two statements above, we get the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(a∣b)\\, & ⇔\\,¬(∃k\\in Z,b=ka) \\\\ a∤b\\, & ⇔\\,∀k\\in Z,b\\ne ka\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $a\\not\\mid b$ means that $b \\neq ak$ *for all* integers $k.$\n\nFor example, $3\\not \\mid 8$ because there does not exist an integer ${\\color{red}{k_2}}$ such that\n\n$$\n8 = {\\color{red}{k_2}}\\cdot 3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163368 #edb/ref 1065 9355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163368 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1a06989c-367e-5d20-840c-e5ab34c82911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163368 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Divisibility" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420027 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420027 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420027 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420027 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420027 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186420078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420027 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420027 #edb/ref 1045 "Integer Divisibility" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420027 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186420076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420027 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420027 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420027 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3f8a0c05-783a-5bb8-ba35-0cfc1728bfdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420028 #edb/ref 1080 "for any" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420028 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420028 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c6cecab-b539-59d3-83dc-06106b4873ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420029 #edb/ref 1080 "$7$ doesn't divide $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420029 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420029 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e80c981-b97a-5513-91b8-2faa41d87131" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420030 #edb/ref 1080 "6\\ne 5k" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420030 #edb/re