20068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420068 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420068 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420068 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420068 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420068 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f06f4411-1ba0-55d0-b599-72a83087bfcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420069 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420069 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186420049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420069 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420069 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420069 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186420034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420069 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f07d0427-ca84-53ef-b241-840f24e28d37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420070 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420070 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420070 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420070 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420070 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420070 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420070 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe notation $4∤7$ means {{field-1}}. It is equivalent to the statement that {{field-2}}{{field-3}} $k,$ we have {{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420070 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ba4131d-53a4-5793-b374-0ddb7648dbe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420070 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∤b$ means $a$ does not divides $b.$ So, $4∤7$ means $4\\text{ doesn't divide }7.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb\\ne ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $4∤7$ is equivalent to the statement that $\\text{for any}$ $\\text{integer}$ $k,$ we have $7\\ne 4k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420071 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420071 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420071 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $3∣(14x+21y)$\n- $7∣(14x+21y)$\n- $4∣(14x+21y)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48fbd1ea-16f2-572a-98a1-c1983af51b24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420071 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n14x+21y=7(2x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $7∣(14x+21y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420072 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420072 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420072 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420072 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $2$ divides $(6x-18y)$\n- $3$ divides $(6x-18y)$\n- $5$ divides $(6x-18y)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "63936aa1-e722-5014-a82a-6fe76f4b851d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420072 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x-18y=2(3x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $3x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2$ divides $(6x-18y).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x-18y=3(2x-6y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x-6y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3$ divides $(6x-18y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=2$ and $y=1,$ we have $6(2)-18(1)=-6,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420073 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420073 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420073 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420073 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186420069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420073 #edb/ref 1069 "q-221920" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420073 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420073 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe notation $5∤6$ means {{field-1}}. It is equivalent to the statement that {{field-2}}{{field-3}} $k,$ we have {{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420073 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8eb0e9a4-7a3a-5999-9897-bbedb74b2220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420073 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $a∤b$ means $a$ does not divides $b.$ So, $5∤6$ means $5\\text{ doesn't divide }6.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb\\ne ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $5∤6$ is equivalent to the statement that $\\text{for any}$ $\\text{integer}$ $k,$ we have $6\\ne 5k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420074 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186420078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420074 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "56eb53ba-c780-5a71-a330-07b5e88ca9a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420074 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186420071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420075 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186420077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420075 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6200d2fa-ec68-5795-8705-de7c6137e85c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420075 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186420070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420076 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186420075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420076 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f90a01d-2bf3-5e73-ae54-8cf83cc03dfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420076 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420077 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186420074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420077 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9fa38660-d8a3-5241-9915-730f9151df80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420077 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186420073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420078 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d7173f03-42ea-5a77-a52b-599bcf37ac02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186420078 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186420072 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
